  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-7518" textLabel="S-allantoin synthase" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-7518" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">|FRAME:CPLX-7518 S-allantoin synthase| from |FRAME: ORG-5993 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; col| is a bifunctional enzyme that catalyzes the last two steps in the pathway converting |FRAME: URATE urate| to |FRAME: S-ALLANTOIN &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-allantoin| based on &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays using a recombinant enzyme |CITS: [20167108][20511299]|. While most organisms have two separate genes for these two enzymes, in |FRAME: ORG-5993 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; col| the genes have fused to encode a single polypeptide |CITS: [17567580][20167108][20511299]|. The decarboxylase portion of the enzyme was amplified and used to produce a stand-alone decarboxylase enzyme. It was crystalized in the presence of |FRAME: S-ALLANTOIN &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-allantoin|, and the crystal structure was solved at a resolution of 2.5 &amp;Aring; |CITS: [17567580]|. The OHCU decarboxylase portion of the protein formed a dimer in this crystal |CITS: [17567580]|. However, later gel filtration chromatography experiments using a recombinant version of the full-length protein suggested that it forms a homotetramer |CITS: [20167108]|. TTL is different from other known decarboxylases in having no metal cofactors. A mechanism explaining the ability of the enzyme to produce only the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt; enantiomer of allantoin has been proposed |CITS: [17567580]|. Two alternatively spliced versions of the TTL transcript give rise to two proteins with different subcellular localizations: a longer peroxisomally-targeted protein (AT5G58220.1) and a slightly shorter cytoplasmic protein (AT5G58220.3). Both have roughly equivalent enzymatic activity &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;, but, allantoin biosynthesis is believed to occur in the peroxisome. These alternative splice forms appear to have been conserved throughout angiosperms. The protein and nucleotide sequences linked to this protein refer to the peroxisomal splice variant (AT5G58220.1) |CITS: [20511299]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-7518" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="hydroxyisourate hydrolase / OHCU decarboxylase" />
      <Property key="ComplexComponents" value="AT5G58220-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="ComponentCount" value="1" />
      <Property key="IsComplex" value="true" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-14388, RXN-14387" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="GOTerms" value="GO:0005777" />
      <Property key="^coefficient" value="4" />
      <Property key="^citations" value="20511299" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="CPLX-*" />
      <Property key="Locations" value="CCI-PEROXISOME-PL" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_17567580" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_20167108" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_20511299" />
    </Group>
